1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Pico_della_Mirandola en.wikipedia...] ; [http://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Pico_della_Mirandola nl.wikipedia... ] ; [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Pico_della_Mir
2 CONC andola de.wikipedia...] ; [http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Pico_della_Mirandola it.wikipedia...] ; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Count '''Giovanni Pico della Mirandola''' (Italian: [dʒoˈvanni ˈpiko della miˈrandola]; 24 February 1463 – 17 November 1494) was an Italian Renaissance philosopher.[1] He is famed for the events of 1486, when at the age of 23, he 
2 CONC proposed to defend 900 theses on religion, philosophy, natural philosophy and magic against all comers, for which he wrote the famous Oration on the Dignity of Man, which has been called the "Manifesto of the Renaissance",[2] and 
2 CONC a key text of Renaissance humanism and of what has been called the "Hermetic Reformation".[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni was born at Mirandola, near Modena, the youngest son of Francesco I, Lord of Mirandola and Count of Concordia (1415–1467), by his wife Giulia, daughter of Feltrino Boiardo, Count di Scandiano.[4] The family had long dwelt
2 CONC  in the Castle of Mirandola (Duchy of Modena), which had become independent in the fourteenth century and had received in 1414 from the Emperor Sigismund the fief of Concordia. Mirandola was a small autonomous county (later, a duc
2 CONC hy) in Emilia, near Ferrara. The Pico della Mirandola were closely related to the Sforza, Gonzaga and Este dynasties, and Giovanni's siblings wed the scions of the hereditary rulers of Corsica, Ferrara, Bologna and Forlì.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Born twenty-three years into his parents' marriage, Giovanni had two much older brothers, both of whom outlived him: Count Galeotto I (1442–1499) continued the dynasty, while Antonio (1444–1501) became a general in the Imperial ar
2 CONC my.[4] The Pico family would reign as dukes until Mirandola, an ally of Louis XIV of France, was conquered by his rival, Joseph I, Holy Roman Emperor, in 1708 and annexed to Modena by Duke Rinaldo d'Este, the exiled male line beco
2 CONC ming extinct in 1747.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni's maternal family was singularly distinguished in the arts and scholarship of the Italian Renaissance. His cousin and contemporary was the poet Matteo Maria Boiardo, who grew up under the influence of his own uncle, the F
2 CONC lorentine patron of the arts and scholar-poet, Tito Vespasiano Strozzi. [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Pico_della_Mirandola '''read more...''']
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Pico_della_Mirandola:
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Giovanni Pico della Mirandola (Italian: [dʒoˈvanni ˈpiko della miˈrandola]; 24 February 1463 – 17 November 1494) was an Italian Renaissance philosopher.[1] He is famed for the events of 1486, when at the age of 23, he propos
2 CONC ed to defend 900 theses on religion, philosophy, natural philosophy and magic against all comers, for which he wrote the famous Oration on the Dignity of Man, which has been called the "Manifesto of the Renaissance",[2] and a key 
2 CONC text of Renaissance humanism and of what has been called the "Hermetic Reformation".[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Family
2 CONT * 2 Education
