2 CONT Alfonso XI of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfonso_XI_de_Castilla)
2 CONT
2 CONT A Status of Alfonso XI is in Algeciras
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI (Salamanca, August 13, 1311 – March 26/27, 1350 in Gibraltar) was the king of Castile and León, the son of Ferdinand IV of Castile and his wife Constance of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is variously known among Castilian kings as the Avenger or the Implacable, and as "He of Salado River." The first two names he earned by the ferocity with which he repressed the disorder of the nobles after a long minority; the
2 CONC  third by his victory in the Battle of Rio Salado over the last formidable Marinid invasion of Spain in 1340.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI never went to the insane lengths of his son Pedro of Castile, but he could be bloody in his methods. He killed for reasons of state without form of trial. He openly neglected his wife, Maria of Portugal, and had an oste
2 CONC ntatious passion for Eleanor of Guzman, who bore him ten children. This set Peter an example which he failed to better. It may be that his early death, during the Great Plague of 1350, at the Siege of Gibraltar, only averted a des
2 CONC perate struggle with Peter, though it was a misfortune in that it removed a ruler of eminent capacity, who understood his subjects well enough not to go too far.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI first married Costanza Manuel of Castile on 1325, but divorced her two years later. His second marriage, on 1328, was to Maria of Portugal, daughter of Alfonso IV of Portugal. She was the mother of his sons Fernando (Va
2 CONC lladolid, 1332 – 1333) and Pedro of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his mistress, Eleanor of Guzman, he had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pedro, 1st Lord of Aguilar de Campoo (1330 - 1338)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Juana, 1st Lady of follar Trastamara (born 1330)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sancho, 1st Lord of Ledesma (1331 - 1343)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henry of Trastamara (1334 - 1379)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fadrique, Master of the Order of Santiago and 1st Lord of Haro; (born 1335)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fernando, 2nd Lord of Ledesma
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Tello, 1st Lord of Aguilar de Campoo (1337-1370)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Juan, 1st Lord of Badajoz and Jerez de la Frontera (1341 - 1359)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sancho of Alburquerque (1342-1375)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pedro (1345 - 1359)
2 CONT
2 CONT After Alfonso's death, his widow Maria had Eleanor arrested and later killed (1).
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1)A history of Spain By Charles Edward Chapman, Rafael Altamira, page 26.
2 CONT
2 CONT Referencias:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfonso_XI_de_Castilla_y_Le%C3%B3n#Descendencia
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI de Castilla y de León, el Justiciero (Salamanca, 13 de agosto de 1311 – Gibraltar, 26 de marzo de 1350), fue rey de Castilla y de León
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendencia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Tras un primer matrimonio no consumado, y posteriormente anulado, con Constanza Manuel, hija del Infante don Juan Manuel; contrajo matrimonio en 1328 con su prima hermana María de Portugal (1313 - 1357), hija de Alfonso IV el Brav
2 CONC o, de la que tuvo dos hijos:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fernando (1332), muerto antes de cumplir un año y;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pedro el Cruel (1334 - 1369), futuro rey de Castilla con el nombre de Pedro I.
2 CONT
2 CONT A partir del año 1329 tuvo diez hijos con Leonor Núñez de Guzmán (1310 - 1351) (tataranieta de Alfonso IX de León):
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pedro Alfonso (1330 - 1338), Señor de Aguilar;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Juana Alfonso (1330 - ¿?), Señora de Trastámara;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sancho Alfonso (1331 - 1343), Señor de Ledesma;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Enrique Alfonso el de Las Mercedes (1333 - 1379), señor de Trastámara, fundador de la Casa de Trastámara y futuro rey de Castilla y de León con el nombre de Enrique II;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fadrique Alfonso (1333 - 1358), gemelo del anterior, Maestre de la Orden de Santiago y Señor de Haro; de quien descienden los Almirantes de Castilla-Duques de Medina de Rioseco (Casa de Enríquez);
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fernando Alfonso (¿? - ¿?), Señor de Ledesma;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Tello Alfonso (1337 - 1370), primer Señor de Aguilar de Campoo y Lara, Conde de Vizcaya y de quien descienden los Marqueses de Aguilar de Campoo;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Juan Alfonso (1341 - 1359), Señor de Badajoz y de Jerez de la Frontera;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sancho Alfonso (1345 - 1352), Conde de Alburquerque;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pedro Alfonso (1345 - 1359), gemelo del anterior.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI of Castile 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Salamanca, August 13, 1311 – Died in Gibraltar, March 26, 1350,  was the king of Castile and León, the son of Ferdinand IV of Castile and his wife Constance of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is variously known among Castilian kings as the Avenger or the Implacable, and as "He of Salado River." The first two names he earned by the ferocity with which he repressed the disorder of the nobles after a long minority; the
2 CONC  third by his victory in the Battle of Rio Salado over the last formidable Marinid invasion of Spain in 1340.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI never went to the insane lengths of his son Pedro of Castile, but he could be bloody in his methods. He killed for reasons of state without form of trial. He openly neglected his wife, Maria of Portugal, and had an oste
2 CONC ntatious passion for Eleanor of Guzman, who bore him ten children. This set Peter an example which he failed to better. It may be that his early death, during the Great Plague of 1350, at the Siege of Gibraltar, only averted a des
2 CONC perate struggle with Peter, though it was a misfortune in that it removed a ruler of eminent capacity, who understood his subjects well enough not to go too far.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI first married Costanza Manuel of Castile on 1325, but divorced her two years later. His second marriage, on 1328, was to Maria of Portugal, daughter of Alfonso IV of Portugal. She was the mother of his sons Fernando (Va
2 CONC lladolid, 1332 – 1333) and Pedro of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his mistress, Eleanor of Guzman, he had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Alfonso of Castile, 1st Lord of Aguilar de Campoo (1330 - 1338). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Juana Alfonso of Castile, 1st Lady of Trastamara (born 1330). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho Alfonso of Castile, 1st Lord of Ledesma (1331 - 1343). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Enrique Alfonso of Castile, 1st Count of Trastamara (1334 - 1379). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fadrique Alfonso of Castile, Master of the Order of Santiago and 1st Lord of Haro; (born 1335). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando Alfonso of Castile, 2nd Lord of Ledesma. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tello Alfonso of Castile, 1st Lord of Aguilar de Campoo (1337-1370). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Alfonso of Castile, 1st Lord of Badajoz and Jerez de la Frontera (1341 - 1359). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho Alfonso of Castile, 1st Count of Alburquerque (1342-1375). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Alfonso of Castile (1345 - 1359). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI de Borgoña, el Justiciero (Salamanca, 13 de agosto de 1311 – Gibraltar, 26 de marzo de 1350), fue rey de Castilla y de León, biznieto de Alfonso X el Sabio. Muerto su padre, Fernando IV, en 1312, se desarrollaron multit
2 CONC ud de disputas entre varios aspirantes al trono, resueltos en 1313 con el acuerdo de Palazuelos. Su abuela Doña María de Molina, Doña Constanza y los infantes don Juan y don Pedro, formaron regencia. Cuando murió Doña Constanza y 
2 CONC tras una campaña militar donde perecieron los infantes en Granada en el año 1319, Doña María quedó como única regente. Al morir en 1321 los infantes don Felipe, don Juan Manuel y don Juan el Tuerto se dividieron el reino mientras 
2 CONC era saqueado por los moros y nobles levantinos. Alfonso, una vez mayor de edad, asumió el trono, consiguiendo durante su reinado el fortalecimiento del poder real, la resolución de los problemas del estrecho de Gibraltar y la conq
2 CONC uista de Algeciras. (See http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfonso_XI)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI (Salamanca, August 13, 1311 – March 26/27, 1350 in Gibraltar) was the king of Castile, León and Galicia the son of Ferdinand IV of Castile and his wife Constance of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is variously known among Castilian kings as the Avenger or the Implacable, and as "He of Salado River." The first two names he earned by the ferocity with which he repressed the disorder of the nobles after a long minority; the
2 CONC  third by his victory in the Battle of Rio Salado over the last formidable Marinid invasion of Iberian Peninsula in 1340.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI never went to the insane lengths of his son Peter of Castile, but he could be bloody in his methods. He killed for reasons of state without form of trial. He openly neglected his wife, Maria of Portugal, and had an oste
2 CONC ntatious passion for Eleanor of Guzman, who bore him ten children. This set Peter an example which he failed to better. It may be that his early death, during the Great Plague of 1350, at the Siege of Gibraltar, only averted a des
2 CONC perate struggle with Peter, though it was a misfortune in that it removed a ruler of eminent capacity, who understood his subjects well enough not to go too far.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI first married Costanza Manuel of Castile on 1325, but divorced her two years later. His second marriage, on 1328, was to Maria of Portugal, daughter of Alfonso IV of Portugal. They had:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ferdinand (Valladolid, 1332 – 1333)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Peter of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his mistress, Eleanor of Guzman, he had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pedro, 1st Lord of Aguilar de Campoo (1330–1338)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Juana, 1st Lady of follar Trastamara (born 1330)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sancho, 1st Lord of Ledesma (1331–1343)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henry of Trastamara (1334–1379)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fadrique, Master of the Order of Santiago and 1st Lord of Haro; (born 1335)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fernando, 2nd Lord of Ledesma
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Tello, 1st Lord of Aguilar de Campoo (1337–1370)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Juan, 1st Lord of Badajoz and Jerez de la Frontera (1341–1359)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sancho, 1st Count of Alburquerque (1342–1375)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pedro (1345–1359)
2 CONT
2 CONT After Alfonso's death, his widow Maria had Eleanor arrested and later killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso XI of Castile
