1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Cesare Borgia, Duca di Valentinois''' (September 13, 1475 – March 12, 1507), Duke of Valentinois was a Spanish-Italian condottiero, lord and cardinal. He was the son of Pope Alexander VI and his long-term mistre
2 CONC ss Vannozza dei Cattanei. He was sibling to Lucrezia Borgia, Gioffre Borgia (Jofré in Valentian), Prince of Squillace, and Giovanni Borgia, duke of Gandia, and half-brother to Don Pedro Luis de Borja and Girolama de Borja, childre
2 CONC n of unknown mothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like nearly all aspects of Cesare Borgia's life, the date of his birth is a subject of dispute. However, it is accepted that he was born in Rome either in 1475 or 1476 to Cardinal Rodrigo de Lanzol y Borja, soon to become Pope Ale
2 CONC xander VI, and his mistress Vannozza de' Cattanei, of whom documents are sparse. The Borgia family originally came from Spain and rose to prominence during the mid 15th century, when Cesare's great uncle Alonso Borgia (1378-1458),
2 CONC  bishop of Valencia, was elected Pope Callixtus III in 1455. Cesare's father, Pope Alexander VI, was the first pope who was openly recognized to have children with a lover.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stefano Infessura writes that Cardinal Borgia falsely claimed Cesare to be the legitimate son of another man, the nominal husband of Vannozza de' Cattanei. More likely, Pope Alexander VI granted Cesare a release from the necessity
2 CONC  of proving his birth in a papal bull.
2 CONT
2 CONT With brown eyes and black hair, Cesare was acknowledged as a beautiful child and grew to be a fleet-footed, tall, handsome man of unlimited ambition, much like his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 10, 1499, Cesare married Charlotte of Albret (1480 - March 11, 1514). She was a sister of John III of Navarre. They were parents to a daughter, Louise Borgia, (1500 - 1553) who first married Louis II de La Tremouille, Gover
2 CONC nor of Burgundy, and secondly Philippe de Bourbon (1499-1557), Seigneur de Busset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cesare was also father to at least 11 illegitimate children, among them Girolamo Borgia, who married Isabella Contessa di Carpi, and Lucrezia Borgia, who, after Cesare's death, was moved to Ferrara to the court of her aunt, Lucrez
2 CONC ia Borgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source: From Wikipedia, the Free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cesare_Borgia
2 CONT
