1 NOTE {geni:about_me} - https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clarice_Orsini
2 CONT
2 CONT Clara Orsini
2 CONT
2 CONT F, #4791, d. circa 1488
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=18 Sep 2002
2 CONT
2 CONT      Clara Orsini was the daughter of James Orsini. She married Lorenzo I de Medici, son of Piero I de Medici, on 20 December 1469. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died circa 1488.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Clara Orsini and Lorenzo I de Medici
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Giuliano de Nemours+1 d. 1516
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. Piero II de Medici+ b. 15 Feb 1471, d. 28 Dec 1503
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p480.htm#i4791
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clarisse_Orsini
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice Orsini
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice Orsini (c. 1453 – 29 July 1487) was the daughter of Jacopo (Giacomo) Orsini (see Orsini family), lord of Monterotondo and Bracciano, and his wife and cousin Maddalena Orsini. Born in Rome, she is most known as the wife of 
2 CONC Lorenzo de' Medici (Lorenzo the Magnificent), de facto ruler of the Florentine Republic. She was the mother of Pope Leo X.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lorenzo and Clarice were married by proxy on 7 February 1469. The marriage was arranged by Lorenzo's mother Lucrezia Tornabuoni, who wanted her eldest son to marry a woman from a noble family, in order to enhance the social status
2 CONC  of the Medicis. Clarice's dowry was 6,000 florins. She arrived in Florence on 4 June 1469.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice was not popular in Florence, because her strict religious personality was in deep contrast with the humanist ideals of the age. Even Lorenzo preferred a Florentine woman, Lucrezia Donati, to whom he dedicated his poems, bu
2 CONC t he had children only from Clarice. Of the nine children born to them, three died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Pazzi Conspiracy, which was aimed at murdering Lorenzo and his younger brother Giuliano, Clarice and her children were sent to Pistoia. (The Pazzis succeeded in murdering Giuliano, but Lorenzo survived the attack, thus 
2 CONC the conspirators' plan to replace the Medicis as de facto rulers of Florence failed.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice returned to Rome several times to visit her relatives; she also visited Volterra, Colle Val d'Elsa, Passignano, and other places in the 1480s. On 29 July 1487 she died unexpectedly from tuberculosis in Florence.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice and Lorenzo's children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Lucrezia (1470–1553), married Jacopo Salviati: their sons were Cardinals Giovanni Salviati and Bernardo Salviati, their daughter Maria Salviati was the mother of Cosimo I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany; another daughter, Fr
2 CONC ancesca Salviati was the mother of Pope Leo XI
2 CONT
2 CONT -2., 3. Twins died after birth (March 1471)
2 CONT
2 CONT -4. Piero (1472–1503), ruler of Florence after his father's death; married Alfonsina Orsini
2 CONT
2 CONT -5. Maddalena (1473–1519), married Franceschetto Cybo, illegitimate son of Pope Innocent VIII
2 CONT
2 CONT -6. Giovanni (1475–1521) became Pope Leo X
2 CONT
2 CONT -7. Luisa (or Luigia; 1477–1488), was betrothed to Giovanni il Popolano, but died young
2 CONT
2 CONT -8. Contessina (1478–1515) married Piero Ridolfi
2 CONT
2 CONT -9. Giuliano (1479–1516), Duke of Nemours, married Filiberta di Savoia
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clarice_Orsini
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice Orsini
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice Orsini (c. 1453 – 29 July 1487) was the daughter of Jacopo (Giacomo) Orsini (see Orsini family), lord of Monterotondo and Bracciano, and his wife and cousin Maddalena Orsini. Born in Rome, she is most known as the wife of 
2 CONC Lorenzo de' Medici (Lorenzo the Magnificent), de facto ruler of the Florentine Republic. She was the mother of Pope Leo X.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lorenzo and Clarice were married by proxy on 7 February 1469. The marriage was arranged by Lorenzo's mother Lucrezia Tornabuoni, who wanted her eldest son to marry a woman from a noble family, in order to enhance the social status
2 CONC  of the Medicis. Clarice's dowry was 6,000 florins. She arrived in Florence on 4 June 1469.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice was not popular in Florence, because her strict religious personality was in deep contrast with the humanist ideals of the age. Even Lorenzo preferred a Florentine woman, Lucrezia Donati, to whom he dedicated his poems, bu
2 CONC t he had children only from Clarice. Of the nine children born to them, three died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Pazzi Conspiracy, which was aimed at murdering Lorenzo and his younger brother Giuliano, Clarice and her children were sent to Pistoia. (The Pazzis succeeded in murdering Giuliano, but Lorenzo survived the attack, thus 
2 CONC the conspirators' plan to replace the Medicis as de facto rulers of Florence failed.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice returned to Rome several times to visit her relatives; she also visited Volterra, Colle Val d'Elsa, Passignano, and other places in the 1480s. On 29 July 1487 she died suddenly from tuberculosis in Florence.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice and Lorenzo's children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucrezia (1470–1553), married Jacopo Salviati: their sons were Cardinals Giovanni Salviati and Bernardo Salviati, their daughter Maria Salviati was the mother of Cosimo I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany; another daughter, France
2 CONC sca Salviati was the mother of Pope Leo XI
2 CONT
2 CONT Twins died after birth (March 1471)
2 CONT
2 CONT Piero (1472–1503), ruler of Florence after his father's death; married Alfonsina Orsini
2 CONT
2 CONT Maddalena (1473–1519), married Franceschetto Cybo, illegitimate son of Pope Innocent VIII
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni (1475–1521) became Pope Leo X
2 CONT
2 CONT Luisa (or Luigia; 1477–1488), was betrothed to Giovanni il Popolano, but died young
2 CONT
2 CONT Contessina (1478–1515) married Piero Ridolfi
2 CONT
2 CONT Giuliano (1479–1516), Duke of Nemours, married Filiberta di Savoia
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT  Clarice Orsini (c. 1453 – 29 July 1487) was the daughter of Jacopo (Giacomo) Orsini (see Orsini family), lord of Monterotondo and Bracciano, and his wife and cousin Maddalena Orsini. Born in Rome, she is most known as the wife of
2 CONC  Lorenzo de' Medici (Lorenzo the Magnificent), de facto ruler of the Florentine Republic. She was the mother of Pope Leo X.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lorenzo and Clarice were married by proxy on 7 February 1469. The marriage was arranged by Lorenzo's mother Lucrezia Tornabuoni, who wanted her eldest son to marry a woman from a noble family, in order to enhance the social status
2 CONC  of the Medicis. Clarice's dowry was 6,000 florins. She arrived in Florence on 4 June 1469.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice was not popular in Florence, because her strict religious personality was in deep contrast with the humanist ideals of the age. Even Lorenzo preferred a Florentine woman, Lucrezia Donati, to whom he dedicated his poems, bu
2 CONC t he had children only from Clarice. Of the nine children born to them, three died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Pazzi Conspiracy, which was aimed at murdering Lorenzo and his younger brother Giuliano, Clarice and her children were sent to Pistoia. (The Pazzis succeeded in murdering Giuliano, but Lorenzo survived the attack, thus 
2 CONC the conspirators' plan to replace the Medicis as de facto rulers of Florence failed.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarice returned to Rome several times to visit her relatives; she also visited Volterra, Colle Val d'Elsa, Passignano, and other places in the 1480s. On 29 July 1487 she died unexpectedly from tuberculosis in Florence.
2 CONT
