1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Michael Johann III, Hereditary Cupbearer of the Holy Roman Empire, Count von Althann (8 Oct 1679 - 16 March 1722)'''
2 CONT s/o Michael Johann von Althann (26 Aug1643 - 1722) &  Princess Maria Theresia von und zu Liechtenstein (10 Aug 1643 - Oct 26, 1712).
2 CONT x  12 Feb 1709, Barcelona ,  Maria Anna Josepha, née Marchesa Pignatelli, Duchess of Belriguardo ( 26 July 1689 -  1 March 1755, Vienna on) 
2 CONT *Michael Johann Gangelsift IV (5 April 1710 -)
2 CONT *Maria Theresia (19 Oct 1711 -9 Feb 1759) x 2 May 1728, Leopold Maria Gundaccar Count von Dietrichstein, lord of Wodernstein
2 CONT *Michael Carl (15 April 1714 - 1745)
2 CONT *Maria Anna Sidonia (1 May 1715 -1773 ) x 3 May 1738, Nicolaus Count von Palffy ab Erdöd
2 CONT *Michael Anton Ignaz (21 July 1716 -)
2 CONT *Maria Anna Josepha (8 Feb 1718 -) who entered the Jesuit order.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann_Michael_von_Althann
2 CONT Johann Michael came from the noble family of Althann . His parents were Michael Johann von Althann (* August 26, 1643; † 1722) and his wife Princess Maria Theresia von und zu Liechtenstein (* August 10, 1643; † October 26, 1712).
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Franz Karl Wißgrill : ''Schaubplatz des landsässigen Niederösterreichischen Adels.'' Frst published in Vienna 1794: 
2 CONT ''Stage of the landed Lower Austrian nobility of the lords and knights from the 11th century onwards, up to present times ,'' Volume 1, pp. 86f. [https://books.google.de/books?id=fD0TAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA86#v=onepage&q&f=false Digitized 
2 CONC version]
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224735428884&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224735571856&size=large}}
2 CONT Michael Johann III, Hereditary Cupbearer of the Holy Roman Empire, Count von Althann, Banner- and Free Lord of Goldburg and Murstetten, Knight of the Golden Fleece, Grandee of Spain of the First Class, lord of the lordship and isl
2 CONC and of Mursták and Beste Eszatsburn in Hungary, as well as of the fideicommiss lordship of Swoyschitz in Bohemia, was born on 8 October 1679.
2 CONT
2 CONT Already in 1703, as Master of the Horse and Royal Chamberlain, he accompanied His Majesty King Charles, Archduke of Austria—later Roman Emperor Charles VI—to Spain. There, throughout all journeys, campaigns, and dangers, he distin
2 CONC guished himself most notably by his loyalty, splendid service, and merits, thereby earning the special highest favor of this monarch. Through the eminent prerogatives, dignities, and imperial rewards he obtained, he greatly increa
2 CONC sed the splendor of his exalted family. From that time onward, he was commonly called by both the public and the nobility “the Spanish Althann.”
2 CONT
2 CONT After returning from Spain, and already having attained the dignity of Grandee of Spain of the First Class, he was in 1712 reliably admitted as Knight of the Golden Fleece, and finally became Chief Master of the Horse to His reign
2 CONC ing Imperial and Royal Majesty. Furthermore, on 19 June 1714, Emperor Charles VI granted him and his male descendants the hereditary barony of the Empire. In 1717, the same Emperor bestowed upon him the princely county of Grabitsc
2 CONC h, which had reverted to the crown after the extinction of the Princes of Eggenberg, and elevated him to Imperial Prince. He himself valued this highly, especially since the possession of the Duchy of Carniola was contested due to
2 CONC  debts of about 200,000 florins attached to Grabitsch, which were raised against its transfer to this county. Thereupon…
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224735540858&size=large}}
2 CONT By virtue of the highest resolution issued to the Court Chamber on 12 May 1719, Emperor Charles granted to Count Michael Johann von Althann and his descendants the considerable fiscal lordship and estate of Murass together with th
2 CONC e attached Castatornya (Eisafsturm) in the Kingdom of Hungary, situated in the Szaladin County on the borders of Croatia and Styria.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in Barcelona on 12 February 1709 Maria Anna Josepha, née Marchesa Pignatelli, Duchess of Belriguardo, who at that time was serving as Lady-in-Waiting to Her Majesty Queen Elisabeth Christina. She was born on 26 July 168
2 CONC 9, became a Dame of the Order of the Starry Cross, and, like her husband, always enjoyed the special highest favor of the Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Count Michael Johann, her husband, had already died at the age of 43 on 16 March 1722, the Emperor granted her, as widow, in the same year the lordship and barony of Rocca-Guillelma in the Kingdom of Naples. Furthermore, by 
2 CONC highest decree dated 26 August 1727, he gifted to her and her sons the former Free House of the Royal Spanish Court Chancellery in Vienna, located in the Vorderen Schenkenstrasse, as their property; the formal imperial donation in
2 CONC strument was issued on 25 October 1727.
2 CONT
2 CONT She made her testament in Vienna on 6 July 1735, in which, among other provisions, she established the aforementioned Free House in Vienna—received as an imperial gift—as a fideicommiss, and appointed her brother, Prince Anton von
2 CONC  Pignatelli, Duke of Belriguardo, Prince of Belmonte, as her testamentary heir. In addition, there exist two codicils dated 27 April 1745 and 16 January 1749, in which she further explained her last will and arranged legacies. She
2 CONC  died in Vienna on 1 March 1755.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224735922835&size=large}}
2 CONT They had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Johann Gangelsift IV, born 5 April 1710, of whom more will be said below.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Theresia, born 19 October 1711, wife of Leopold Maria Gundaccar Count von Dietrichstein, lord of Wodernstein, Stips, Sondberg, etc., Imperial Privy Councillor and Chamberlain; married 12 May 1728. She became a Dame of the Or
2 CONC der of the Starry Cross in the same year, and died 9 February 1759.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Carl, born 15 April 1714, served as a captain (Rittmeister) in Imperial military service, and died unmarried in 1745.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Anna Sidonia, born 11 May 1715, wife of Nicolaus Count von Palffy ab Erdöd, Knight of the Golden Fleece, Imperial Privy Councillor, President of the Court War Council, as well as Judex Curiae (Chief Judge) in the Kingdom of 
2 CONC Hungary; married 3 May 1738, died a widow in 1773.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Anton Ignaz, born 21 July 1716, of whom mention will be made immediately afterwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Anna Josepha, born 8 February 1718, who entered the Jesuit order.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224735877827&size=large}}
2 CONT Count von Althann III was at first a Knight of the Order of Malta, then for several years a captain (Rittmeister) in the Postonir Regiment, afterwards Imperial Chamberlain, and finally Imperial Privy Councillor, General of Cavalry
2 CONC , Colonel and proprietor of an Imperial Dragoon Regiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited from his uncle, Count Michael Hermann Joseph von Althann, the lordships of Jaslowitz, Frain, and Mühlgrub in Moravia. In his testament, drawn up in Vienna on 30 October 1774 and recorded in the Lower Austrian Land Reg
2 CONC ister, he established these estates as a fideicommiss for his universal heir, Count Michael Joseph von Althann, son of his brother Michael Johann.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died after a short illness on 1 November 1774 in Vienna.
2 CONT ----
